WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.730
Our next two speakers
are Melanie Martin.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.030
She was a postdoctoral associate
in biological anthropology

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.440
at Yale.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.160
She studies growth and nutrition
among indigenous populations

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.730
in Bolivia and Argentina
and registered her most

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.759
recent project on the
open science framework.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.070
Melanie holds a BA
from the University

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:40.290
of Puerto Rico and an MA and PhD
from the UC of Santa-- UC Santa

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.260
Barbara.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.670
She holds a certificate in
college and university teaching

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.310
and has taught courses
in human variation,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.950
evolutionary medicine,
and statistical methods

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.050
for the behavioral sciences.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.360
Her co-presenter
is Brett Beheim is

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.820
a senior researcher at
the Max Planck Institute

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.010
for Evolutionary Anthropology.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.470
He studies cultural and
technological change

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.410
using both large scale
economic data sets

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.070
and ethnographic field work
from small scale societies.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.250
He has shared several
research projects on GitHub.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.570
Professor Beheim holds a BS
from Emory University and a PhD

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.360
from the University of
California at Los Angeles.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.440
Welcome both of you.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.430
OK.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.910
So thank you to Michelle
and the other organizers

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.470
and presenters.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.170
I also want to
thank our colleagues

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.980
in the Yale department-- Eduardo
Fernandez-Duque, Margaret

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.760
Coralee, and Juan Pablo
Perea who together with Brett

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.480
we've formed sort
of a working group

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.320
to discuss analytical
methods in anthropology that

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.130
have relevance to questions
about transparency

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.800
and reproducibility in
observational research, which

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.480
we'll both be
talking about today.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.390
So to start off,
I actually wanted

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.240
to explain some of the
research objectives

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.150
in quantitative anthropology,
because most people are

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.840
familiar with anthropology
as ethnographic studies

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.250
of foreign cultures, which are
very important and critical

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.320
to current anthropology.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.110
But there are also
many researchers

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.680
who are studying cultural
and biological processes

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.490
in a systematic fashion,
studying human and non-human

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.200
primate evolution and
behavior, and then,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.470
of course, fossil and
archaeological remains

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.860
from our ancestral
and more recent past.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:02.210
And in studying these topics,
we use theory and methods

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.420
from a host of natural
and social sciences

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.030
along with quantitative
methods of data collection

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.870
and analysis.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.410
So some of this
research lends itself

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.220
very easily to open,
transparent, and reproducible

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.630
methods.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:22.980
For example, a recent study
published by Cody Ross--

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.920
an anthropologist
now at Max Planck--

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.920
he examined evidence of racial
bias and police shootings using

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.090
a publicly available database
of US police shootings

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.070
that's actually crowdsourced
and maintained by Deadspin.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:43.230
Some of the key
findings of this study

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.590
was that racial bias
was more prevalent

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.920
in certain large
metropolitan areas,

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.380
but wasn't really correlated
with local crime rates.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.730
Instead it seemed to be more
predicted by higher income

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.000
inequality in those areas.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.030
He also found that on
average across counties

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.670
black and unarmed Americans
were 3 and 1/2 times

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:10.710
as likely to be shot by police
as white and unarmed Americans.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.270
So in addition to the
importance of these findings,

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:17.510
this is a wonderful illustration
of open, reproduceable research

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:18.570
in anthropology.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.734
Because not only does the
data come from a raw--

00:04:21.734 --> 00:04:25.050
does the raw data come from a
publicly available database,

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:30.600
but Ross also made available
in the publication the cleaned

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.460
and compiled data
sets that he used

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.020
in the study and all of his code
for conducting the analysis.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.330
So that said though,
most quantitative

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.610
anthropological
research is still

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.540
done by individual researchers
or small groups of researchers

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.330
working with data that
they collect often

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.300
in small remote populations.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:56.010
So an example of that would be
a recent study from Brian Wood--

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.590
an anthropologist here
at Yale-- and colleagues

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.620
who looked at physical activity
and cardiovascular disease

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.730
risk among Hadza
hunter-gatherers in Tanzania.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:10.770
And what they found was that
the Hadza are about 14 times as

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.660
likely to spend
their day engaged

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.900
in moderate to vigorous
physical activity as

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.080
compared to same
aged US subjects.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.640
You can also see in this
plot from that study--

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.400
the blue line are males
and the green line

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.980
are females-- that activity
levels aren't lower with age.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.660
In fact, some of
the older adults

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.670
had relatively higher
activity levels,

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:36.480
which is not a trend that
we typically see in the US.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.570
They also found in
a separate study

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:45.000
that across ages in the Hadza
we see a very low prevalence

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.700
of hypertension and
other biomarkers

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.620
of cardiovascular disease
risk, such as LDL cholesterol,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.390
high c-reactive protein,
and high triglycerides.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:00.630
Similar results have been shown
and other subsistent scale

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.700
indigenous populations
and together

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.850
contribute to our growing
understanding that many

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:10.320
of the chronic diseases that
plague citizens in our country

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.840
and other westernized
nations, including

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.020
cardiovascular disease risk,
obesity, diabetes, and cancer--

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.720
these are really diseases
of modern civilization.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:22.530
And we just don't see
them in populations

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:26.400
that still engage in
relatively ancestral behaviors

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.670
with high activity levels and
relatively low caloric intake.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.210
These are some pictures from the
data collection in that study.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.420
So you can see subjects
going about their daily lives

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.260
climbing trees to collect
honey, digging for tubers,

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.760
and just hanging out.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.520
And all of these subjects
have heart rate monitors

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.380
and GPS monitors
strapped to them,

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.170
which the researchers
used to actually get

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.480
the data about their
physical activity.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.550
And I illustrate
these really just

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:00.330
to show you that this data
is really hard to get.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.070
Because first off,
they're working

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.870
in a remote area of Tanzania.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.980
This is also only
possible because Dr. Wood

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.200
and his colleagues
have spent decades

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:13.890
building trust and understanding
with this population.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.740
And on top of that,
they're collecting

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.430
electronic and biomarker
data in an area

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:24.000
without electricity or
internet or refrigeration.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.460
And then on top of all those
logistical difficulties,

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.410
if you have ever
done field work,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.590
you know that in the field,
everything goes wrong

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.090
and nothing goes to plan.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.610
And you have to work
from plan B, C, D,

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:41.160
and change your original
design concept multiple times.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.240
So for this reason,
anthropological research really

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.030
is often constrained
to very small data sets

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:51.810
or small populations with
one shot data collection

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:55.050
that also has to be very
flexible at the same time.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.860
So I think this raises
questions for what

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.580
transparent and reproducible
research in anthropological

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.600
and other field
studies really is.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.600
So to examine that,
we might start

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.720
by first acknowledging what
observational research can

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.720
and can't do.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:17.070
So first off, really,
we don't usually

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.070
have experimental controls,
because these may be

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.860
impossible and often unethical.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.150
Second, even if you might be
working with a small sample

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:30.470
size or actually the sample
size is the entire population

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:35.450
in the case if you're working
with available skeletal sample.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:38.030
Also, often many
times, our populations

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:41.049
are specific to a
certain time and place.

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:42.890
It might be rapidly
changing, which

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:45.800
is happening with many
subsistence level cultures

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.360
like the Hazda.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.550
And in that sense, then
anthropological research--

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.260
anthropological
researchers we can never

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.450
resample our populations.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:59.510
And in that sense, our data
can never be replicated.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.070
OK.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.710
Secondly, we might
really examine

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.320
what the goals of
observational research are.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:16.440
And this has been
echoed earlier today too

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:21.210
in terms of acknowledging that
very often, we are exploring

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.990
and describing a known
phenomena and generating

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.950
new hypotheses from the
patterns that we observe.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.080
At the same time,
researchers might

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.720
be testing existing hypotheses
from existing theories,

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.350
but using proxies in
observational research

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.600
rather than
experimental controls.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.280
That said, even though we lack
experimental controls to show

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.360
pure causality, we are
still interested in showing

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.970
empirical support
for these hypotheses

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.560
and establishing
robust associations,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:00.240
as these findings then
can inform future research

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.330
and policy in certain cases.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:13.830
OK.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:19.470
So on top of acknowledging
these challenges and goals that

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.230
are sometimes specific
to observational

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:25.410
as opposed to experimental
research, anthropology

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:26.910
is-- anybody who
might be familiar

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:30.690
with the history of Margaret
Mead's research might know

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.700
there's a long
history of questioning

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.300
methods and transparency
in anthropology.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.980
So thinking about
all these things,

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.840
we have tried to identify
certain practices that

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.950
might promote transparency and
reproducibility in anthropology

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.780
and other observational studies.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:52.170
So with that, I'm going
to hand the talk over

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:57.670
to my co-presenter, who is going
to discuss some of these areas,

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:03.180
again, that have been mentioned
today in terms of identifying

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.580
and using appropriate
statistical methods

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.820
for exploratory versus
confirmatory analysis

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:12.300
and emphasizing
reproducible methods rather

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.080
than replicability of results
through data management

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:19.260
and sharing, registering
analytical protocols, and then

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.928
internal incentives to
promote this behavior.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:25.020
Yes.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:25.770
Thank you.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.930
As Melanie described,
in anthropology

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:33.060
to paraphrase Monty Python,
every data point is precious.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.010
And often the
information that we

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:45.272
have about a particular site
or historical location--

00:11:45.272 --> 00:11:47.134
is that OK?

00:11:47.134 --> 00:11:49.800
Often the information we have is
possible-- the only information

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:53.230
that will ever be known about
that particular population.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:56.310
So we have a somewhat
unique situation.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.950
But in anthropology, as most
of the physical and natural

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:02.610
sciences, there's active
interest in the open science

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.620
and reproducibility
movement that I

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:09.310
think has kind of been
percolating up here.

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:14.180
So in terms of these
statistical aspects

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.910
of quantitative
anthropology, there's

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:20.510
a interest-- a renewed interest
in developing methodologies

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:22.580
that are suited towards
observational studies

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:24.220
with small samples.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.960
And often, this is
predicated on the idea

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.930
that the traditional
methods in statistics

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:32.690
were often developed in
situations that we can't really

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.820
apply to the kinds of analyzes
and data sets we're using.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:37.880
Often, they were
developed in the context--

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:41.415
and in the case of many frequent
statistics-- in strict control

00:12:41.415 --> 00:12:43.790
treatment experimental studies,
where we have the ability

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.940
to model exactly how the
analysis is going to happen

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:49.730
before the data is collected.

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:52.250
In anthropological research--
observational research

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:56.270
in anthropology-- often
we forego the direct goal

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:59.614
of establishing causality
or treatment effects,

00:12:59.614 --> 00:13:01.280
and focus instead on
trying to establish

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.790
reliable or robust associations
between measurements.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:07.230
And there's also a
small difference,

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:09.560
but I think a very important
one between arguing

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:12.230
that we're arguing for
a causal mechanism,

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:14.900
as opposed to predicting
a particular phenomenon

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.060
without necessarily
making direct claims

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:19.250
about the causality.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:21.260
This is, of course,
the same idea

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:24.350
when it comes to Bayesian
modeling techniques applied

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:26.579
to predicting outcomes
that-- oh God, I've

00:13:26.579 --> 00:13:27.870
got to get it up on the screen.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.300
Sorry.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:33.140
So much like election
predictions and Moneyball--

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:42.230
sorry flashbacks-- and
the new wave of analyzes

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:44.360
that we see in social
science, often this

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:47.810
takes on tools drawing
from Bayesian approaches

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:50.164
from information theory,
machine learning methods as

00:13:50.164 --> 00:13:52.080
described, for example,
in Hastie, Tibshirani,

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.270
and Friedman.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:54.980
These are all
techniques which are

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.560
coming in to
anthropological analyzes

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:00.047
just as much as they are
into other social sciences.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.070
One other aspect, which comes up
quite a bit with us especially

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.050
in anthropology,
which we think maybe

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.330
as we're talking
the most about here,

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.340
is the difference
between exploratory and

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:19.600
hypothesis-oriented research, or
is the way that Brian Nosek put

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.910
it yesterday in
his talk-- thinking

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:25.570
about looking at
something prospectively

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.430
as an exploratory approach
or as a justification,

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:31.240
scrutinizes any existing theory.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.910
And this is another great
paper by Paul Rosenbaum,

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:35.830
which was written
specifically for psychology.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.460
But I think it's applicable
to anthropology as well,

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.260
as a justification
for what we can

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:44.440
call nontraditional types
of papers, papers that

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.252
are oriented around
exploring data

00:14:47.252 --> 00:14:51.700
sets in a way that could
be described negatively

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.370
as data dredging or harking,
which is hypothesizing

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.440
after results
known-- both of which

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.070
have been become relatively
recently identified

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:05.800
as serious problems in behind
the non-reproducibility plague

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:09.250
that seems to be
happening under our noses.

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:11.170
But from an anthropological
point of view,

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:12.430
this is often all we can do.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.470
We don't have necessarily
strong hypotheses

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.050
coming into a particular
field site or location.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:20.170
And we struggle
with the framework

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:24.310
of a strict hypothesis
testing sort of idiom,

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:26.020
simply because in
many cases, it's

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.570
not appropriate, or
the analysis itself

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:30.910
motivates particular
relationships

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:32.530
between variables.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:34.840
So how to navigate that
with the obvious problems

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.970
with things like harking
is something that I think

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:41.530
is worth talking about more.

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:43.399
I will mention one
particular study

00:15:43.399 --> 00:15:45.940
that I was part of that came
out this year, because I thought

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.120
that it had a relatively
novel approach at least

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.680
in anthropology
to doing the data

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:52.570
analysis and the presentation.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.180
And as Melanie mentioned,
sometimes the reproducibility

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.070
of the methods is possibly
the best we can do,

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.630
because we're dealing with field
populations or with historical

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:04.930
archaeological data, which
cannot be replicated.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:09.430
We cannot do another study from
the same source and collect

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:11.560
more data from that source.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.060
So this is a
particular project that

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:15.430
was spearheaded by Siobhan
Mattison at the University

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:19.480
of New Mexico collecting
data on parental investment

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.250
and decision making
in a minority

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.410
group in southwest China.

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:26.920
And the particular research
question that she proposed was

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:30.640
is there evidence that
women facultatively

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:32.802
change their reproductive
stopping behavior?

00:16:32.802 --> 00:16:34.510
That is, the number
of children that they

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:37.000
will have conditional
on the current sex

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:41.265
composition of their
children that they have.

00:16:41.265 --> 00:16:43.390
So the way that we visualize
the particular results

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:46.450
of this model-- we
have two different sets

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:48.410
of communities within
this population--

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:52.000
the Moso of southwest China.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.670
And these sort of
honeycomb shapes

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:58.630
here represent a sequence
of birth decision making,

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:01.060
depending on the existing
number of children

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:03.316
and how many children
are boys versus girls.

00:17:03.316 --> 00:17:05.440
So starting at the top of
each one of the pyramids,

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:08.530
we imagine a woman
with no children

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.020
having a probability of
continuing reproduction--

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.420
the probability of having
another child-- the parity

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:15.940
progression ratio as it's
called in demography.

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:19.980
And the orange pyramid starts
at 93% and then the blue pyramid

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.000
88.9%.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.319
I'll explain what the
colors mean in a second.

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:26.290
And then conditional on having
a particular child, either a boy

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:28.510
or girl, we moved
down the pyramid

00:17:28.510 --> 00:17:30.700
into one of the
corresponding cells.

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:33.020
And we have another stopping
probability-- or sorry--

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:36.400
a continuation probability--
the probability of continuing on

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:39.037
to have another child.

00:17:39.037 --> 00:17:40.995
The stratification here
between the patrilineal

00:17:40.995 --> 00:17:43.829
and the matrilineal
is theoretically

00:17:43.829 --> 00:17:45.370
interesting in this
particular study,

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:48.310
because we have reasons
to think that groups where

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:51.700
property and status and
titles are inherited

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:53.620
through the mother's
line might have

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:56.740
a different view of the marginal
value of having another child

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:58.800
if it's going to
be a boy or a girl

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:01.510
than on the matrilineal
side of things.

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.190
And in this
particular society, we

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:05.800
have both kinship systems
taking place simultaneously

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:08.109
in different villages in
the same ethnic group.

00:18:08.109 --> 00:18:10.150
So there's a lot of
similarities between the two,

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:11.920
except for a very
marked difference

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:13.150
in their kinship systems.

00:18:13.150 --> 00:18:15.850
And indeed, we do see as you
can see by the colorations

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:20.020
here, a relatively strong
implication that women with two

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:21.910
sons-- or sorry, excuse
me-- two daughters

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.460
in the patrilineal group
have a much higher chance

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:26.590
of continuing on and
having a third child

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:30.190
than in the matrilineal
group having two sons--

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:32.097
or sorry-- two daughters.

00:18:32.097 --> 00:18:34.180
So the reason that I mention
this particular study

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:37.160
is that we have the data
and all of the materials,

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.670
including the R code,
that this was done in R,

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:42.700
available in GitHub and the link
being down at the bottom there.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:45.940
And the paradigm that
we're working here

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:50.440
is using language from the open
source software environments,

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:54.440
which I think there's a lot of
idiomatic similarities here.

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:56.560
So this is a potential
visualization

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:59.920
of a research project done under
strict version control system,

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.710
like using [? Git, ?]
for example.

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:03.440
And I think there's
a lot of similarities

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:06.875
in this picture to what we
saw during Brian's talk.

00:19:06.875 --> 00:19:09.530
Each of these nodes represents
a particular snapshot

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:12.650
of a project continuing
on from the initialization

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:15.890
of the project all the way to
publication or post publication

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:18.500
peer review feedback
on some kind

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:22.610
of potentially Amazon
comments type system,

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:25.280
like we heard
during Alan's talk.

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:28.880
And the contention
that I have here

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:31.150
is that this kind
of framework is--

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:34.880
at least in terms of laying out
how a project might develop--

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.520
is compelling for us as
anthropologists, because it

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:42.380
implies there's a chronological
sequence that's stored,

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:43.760
that can be shared.

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:46.370
This particular
visualization here also

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:50.060
has different branches
that correspond to-- we

00:19:50.060 --> 00:19:51.500
could imagine--
branches that were

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.140
willing to be made public--
maybe the blue line at the top

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:55.610
there, the master branch.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:58.130
And then other components
during the development process

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:01.640
that for reasons of
confidentiality or sensitivity,

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.100
we don't have the
ability to disclose

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:09.110
or we don't have as
available for public release.

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:12.590
And this is maybe the last
point that, in anthropology

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:16.580
particular, it's very often
the case that anthropologists

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:18.800
see themselves not
only as researchers

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.020
for particular population,
but also as advocates

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:21.860
for that population.

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:23.781
There's usually a very
large power difference

00:20:23.781 --> 00:20:25.280
between the groups
that we work with

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:26.960
and ourselves as researchers.

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:30.170
And so that informs the way
we look at data releases.

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:33.080
And I can say, just speaking
from my own ethnographic

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:35.480
experience with
anthropology, we generally

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:37.139
are very much data hoarders.

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:38.930
And part of the reason
why is because we're

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:40.910
afraid there's going to
be unintended consequences

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:43.284
of making too much information
available up the groups we

00:20:43.284 --> 00:20:45.050
work with.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.540
So in that case, in
this particular project

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.815
with the Moso, I felt that the
solution or the happy medium

00:20:50.815 --> 00:20:52.940
was that we strictly had
the variables and the data

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:55.550
points that were used for the
analysis available with nothing

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:56.050
else.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.670
And to some extent, scrambled
so that they couldn't

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:00.822
be used to match up
to individual people

00:21:00.822 --> 00:21:02.780
without distorting any
of the signals that were

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:05.400
done during the data analysis.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:09.510
So with that, I think we
should mention two things that

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:11.030
are sort of
developments here that I

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:13.550
think are very hopeful or
insightful in anthropology.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:16.757
Mel is responsible for
one of the first projects

00:21:16.757 --> 00:21:19.340
in the open source framework in
anthropology-- pre-registering

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:24.560
and analysis she's doing right
now on the Age at Menarche.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:28.040
And also, to again,
plug the badges,

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:31.070
which is, of course, responsible
from the other open source

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:33.140
framework innovation here.

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:36.940
This is something which
anthropologists here at Yale

00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:39.020
have advocated for
in the International

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:41.480
Journal of Primatology.

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:44.440
And you can see
the citation here.

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:45.050
So yeah.

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:46.820
In conclusion, we
think that there's

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:50.134
a lot of scope for these
ideas in anthropology.

00:21:50.134 --> 00:21:52.550
But there's some concerns or
qualifications that are maybe

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:54.620
unique to our particular field.

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:56.000
And thank you.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.150
[APPLAUSE]

